.TH CAT 1 "September 19, 2026" maux
.SH NAME
cat \- concatenate and print
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cat
.RI [ file ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Cat
reads each file in sequence and writes it on the standard output stream.
Thus:
.PP
.B cat file
.PP
is about the easiest way to print a file. Also:
.PP
.B cat file1 file2 >file3
.PP
is about the easiest way to concatenate files.
If no input file is given,
.I cat
reads from the standard input file.
Bytes are copied without changing their representation.
A file which cannot be opened is reported and skipped.
.SH FILES
/usr/man/CAT  this page
.SH "SEE ALSO"
cp(1), tee(1), licenses(1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Exit status is 0 on success, 1 if an input file cannot be opened.
.SH BUGS
No options are implemented. A named file is not the same as an option; use no operands to read standard input.
